s and  kind of gotten into a routine and it was  interesting because it was the first  time i felt better like bittersweet  about leaving for a tour i was like oh  like i'm gonna miss my friends that i  didn't have before like i had no friends  before and now i have two like it was  really exciting to be able to miss  something and so  um yeah i got very used to my life but  i'm very happy to leave it behind  because i was getting sick of it i was  like oh god yeah my stuff i have so much  when you're young i mean you are young  but when you're young young young and  you start wanting to do this for a  living  the the the words world tour you sort of  like kick around in your head when  you're that's a dream right like that's  one of those things that you want to be  able to say goodbye to everybody you  know guess where i'm going world tour  what is it like to actually sort of live  that  i mean it's  bizarre it is a dream definitely um but  it's a dream in the sense of like just  simply surreal like yeah i don't think  i'll ever wake up and be like i'm on  tour you should though man  i definitely don't think of life that  way um i'm mostly just like stunned and  my friends are like why why does anyone  care that you exist and i'm like i don't  know i don't know either um i think  that's the fun of it is letting myself  really just take in how  completely ridiculous this is let's talk  about jigsaw where was your head out  where was your heart at what's the story  behind this song jigsaw is a song that i  wrote just for myself like a venting  song um about being insecure and i never  talk about being insecure i'm the kind  of person that's very much like when i  feel bad i'm like oh yeah like i'm great  look at me i'm awesome like i feel  perfectly fine  um and so i was pretty scared to release  it i think it came from  a space of just like there's people in  your life who really make you feel like  you're so  undeserving of love and make you feel  like you have to be something in order  to be  worth something and i wanted to write a  song to kind of remind people like hey  you as you are is great  perfect yeah you can sit down and write  that and be vulnerable  and write a great song alone and you can  feel great afterwards but what's the  feeling of having done it and then you  realize that now you have to share it  it's a terrifying feeling um i think  with my debut with kid crow i didn't  have that thought because i'd really not  released much music so i was like oh  yeah whatever like i'll say whatever i  want to say yeah and now i'm like  i really am going to say so much more  than i said on my debut and it was a  terrifying thought and i was mortified  to release songs there were so many  songs that i was like no like it's just  not very good like don't put it on the  album guys like it's whatever like it's  not good just don't put it on um and  they're like no you you have to put this  out i'm like no it's fine um  so i think um it was a arduous process  um  i definitely think of this past album  making process have been like scraping  my ribs for information instead of  my first album was just like me saying  hello you know  heartache heartbreak insecurity we know  that throughout the history of art those  things have led to some fantastic  pieces of art do you ever find that you  sort of move towards  things that maybe aren't the best for  you thinking that there might be  something great at the end of it  yes my  uh me and my friend's  new motto is  it's good for the plot there you go  sometimes we'll like text each other be  like hey like should i see this person  and they're like probably not but  just it's good for the plot like i think  um it's important to make mistakes and i  very much was  a very cautious child and i'm still very  cautious and i've kind of had to retrain  myself to like  allow myself to be heartbroken because i  think it's more worthwhile to feel  emotions that are terrible than feel  nothing at all  which is something i learned from my  youth of just being like terrified to to  do anything do the people who have  broken your heart when they listen to  your music know that they're the ones  who broke your heart  yes for sure they definitely do and a  lot of people who  just assume that the songs are about  them i'm like you wish no  um yeah a lot of uh people conspiring  about  what is about what um but the people the  people who the songs were actually  written about they know do you tell them  ahead of time no no i just let them find  out wow like whatever it did me wrong  did me wrong  and so when you write something that is  so personal and then you do a show and  you see how that song means not only  something to but also something  different to the people in the audience  does it change the way you look at a  song  i mean nothing is more therapeutic  than writing this like miserable  personal song and then  singing it on tour for months of your  life to like screaming screaming smiling  faces um it really changes the way you  think of that experience and so i think  of like heather and how that song was  like the most personal personal  miserable song to write and now every  night it's the most fun and i'm like  wish i were heather like super fun and  it's um it's a very therapeutic thing  and it does change over time for sure  what is it like being at this stage of  your career and having a friend like  olivia rodrigo i mean so important it's  such a confusing  thing um and very exciting and it's just  nice to have people that you can relate  to um so when you  see when you see somebody because i  don't even know her but watching what  happened to her last january when that  track came out and it shook the world  she went global like that um having seen  the way she handled it did it change the  way you sort of deal with being famous  i mean i think that  she is just so talented and was able to  really kind of um  take this great  burden on her 